TI  - Effects of 360-Degree Immersive Virtual Reality Dementia Education on Nursing 
      Students' Knowledge, Attitudes, and Empathy Toward People With Dementia.
LID - e01441 [pii]
LID - 10.1097/CIN.0000000000001441 [doi]
AB  - This randomized controlled trial aimed to evaluate the effects of a dementia 
      education using 360-degree immersive virtual reality (VR) videos on the 
      knowledge, attitudes, and empathy toward people with dementia among nursing 
      students. Fifty eligible nursing students were randomly assigned to VR and 
      control groups. The VR group (n=25) engaged in 3 sessions of 360-degree immersive 
      dementia VR videos featuring clinical scenarios with debriefings and 
      instructor-led group discussions in addition to the conventional lecture-based 
      dementia care course, whereas the control group (n=25) received 3 sessions of 
      instructor-led group discussions on dementia case scenarios without exposure to 
      VR in addition to the conventional course. Data were collected at baseline, week 
      8, and week 18. The VR group showed greater dementia knowledge than the control 
      group at T1 ( P =.015) and T2 ( P =.001), as measured by the Dementia Knowledge 
      Assessment Scale. Empathy, measured by the Jefferson Scale of Empathy, showed 
      significant improvement at T 2 ( P <.001) between groups. However, the Approaches 
      to Dementia Questionnaire results showed no significant improvement in attitudes 
      over time between groups. These findings suggest that a dementia education using 
      360-degree immersive VR videos filmed from the perspectives of people with 
      dementia is an effective teaching strategy for improving nursing students' 
      dementia knowledge and empathy. This study findings may provide valuable insights 
      for the development of dementia care curricula using VR technology in nursing 
      education.
