TI  - Facilitating communication between persons living with dementia and their 
      caregivers: Results of the VRx@home pilot study.
PG  - 1-16
LID - 10.1080/17483107.2026.2705752 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE: Virtual reality (VR) interventions can improve the well-being of persons 
      living with dementia (PLwD). However, little is known about the value of VR 
      interventions on enhancing communication between PLwD and their caregivers in an 
      at-home setting, which was the aim of this study. We also explored whether 
      immersive VR differs from tablet-based technology. MATERIALS AND METHODS: 
      Following baseline data collection, PLwD and caregivers completed a 4-week 
      intervention by watching 360-degree videos together, either starting with the VR 
      or Tablet-only condition (2 weeks per device). Seven dyads participated in the 
      study (N = 14). RESULTS: Data of the five dyads who completed the study indicated 
      that both devices facilitated communication, though the extent varied by outcome 
      measure. Both devices had similar influence on observed interactions and 
      reminiscence, but VR led to a higher frequency of conversation initiations 
      compared to the tablet. CONCLUSIONS: Together, our results demonstrate the 
      promising application of these technologies for building connections and 
      supporting communication in PLwD and caregivers at home.
