TI  - Embodied cognition in neurodegenerative disorders: What do we know so far? A 
      narrative review focusing on the mirror neuron system and clinical applications.
PG  - 66-72
LID - S0967-5868(22)00035-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jocn.2022.01.028 [doi]
AB  - Embodied cognition is a theoretical perspective that considers every form of 
      human knowledge and cognition "embodied", as they pass through bodily experience. 
      The aim of this narrative review is to investigate the importance of mirror 
      neurons system in EC, as well as the EC role in neurodegenerative diseases. This 
      narrative review shows deep connections between body and mind: body states 
      influence mental functions such as perception and reasoning, while mental states 
      cause changes in the body, especially in neurodegenerative disorders. Indeed, 
      abnormalities in EC were found in dementia, Parkinson's Disease and Amyotrophic 
      Lateral sclerosis, also in the absence of other cognitive deficits, negatively 
      affecting patients' outcomes. Exploiting EC mechanisms for rehabilitation 
      purposes, also using innovation technologies, could be a promising therapeutic 
      way to increase motor and cognitive outcomes in patients affected by different 
      neurological disorders.
